s-up play\n- K-J offsuit has approximately 57-60% equity against a random hand in heads-up\n\n**Position & Action:**\n- My opponent checked (action: 0), meaning they called the big blind\n- I'm in the big blind position, so I act after seeing their check\n- This is early in the tournament (hand 2 of 20)\n- I'm slightly ahead in cumulative chips (+500)\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n\n1. **Check (0):** See a free flop with a playable hand. Conservative but reasonable given we're early in the tournament and have position advantage post-flop.\n\n2. **Raise (200+):** Build the pot with a strong hand. K-J offsuit plays well heads-up, and a raise shows strength. A standard raise would be 2-3x the big blind (200-300 chips).\n\n3. **All-in (-2):** Too aggressive for this hand this early. We're only hand 2/20, and K-J offsuit isn't strong enough to risk everything pre-flop.\n\n4. **Fold (-1):** Unreasonable - we have a playable hand and already invested the big blind.\n\n**Opponent's Check:** Could indicate weakness, a trap with a premium hand, or a medium hand wanting to see the flop cheaply.\n\nGiven that K-J is strong in heads-up play and my opponent showed weakness by checking, I should raise to:\n- Build the pot with a good hand\n- Put pressure on potentially weaker holdings\n- Take control of the hand\n\nA raise of 200 (minimum) or 300 would be standard.\n\nReason: K-J offsuit is a strong heads-up hand with good equity. My opponent's check suggests they don't have a premium hand. Raising here allows me to build the pot, gather information, and potentially win immediately if they fold. It's early enough in the tournament that I can apply pressure without excessive risk.\n\nAnswer: 300"}
